# Unity C# naming

Based on [Unity's C# style guide](https://unity.com/resources/c-sharp-style-guide-unity-6) and [naming article](https://unity.com/how-to/naming-and-code-style-tips-c-scripting-unity).

## Identifiers

An **identifier** is any name for a type (class, interface, struct, delegate, enum), member, variable, or namespace. C# cannot use spaces in names; use PascalCase or camelCase for compound phrases.

- **ASCII letters, digits, underscore only** — no backslashes, symbols, or Unicode (breaks some Unity command-line tools).
- **C# only**: camelCase / PascalCase — not `snake_case`, `kebab-case`, or Hungarian (`strName`). **UI Toolkit UXML/USS classes**: BEM (`block__element--modifier`) — see `unity-ui-toolkit`.
- **Readable word order**: `HorizontalAlignment` over `AlignmentHorizontal`; `CanScrollHorizontally` over `ScrollableX`.
- **Pronounceable and searchable**; no joke or pun names (`infiniteMonkeys`).

## Conventions

| Kind | Convention | Example |
|------|------------|---------|
| Types | PascalCase noun/phrase | `PlayerController` |
| Interfaces | `I` + PascalCase | `IDamageable` |
| Public fields / properties | PascalCase, no prefix | `MaxHealth` |
| Private instance fields | `m_` + camelCase | `m_movementSpeed` |
| Static fields | `s_` + camelCase | `s_instance` |
| Constants | `k_` + PascalCase | `k_DefaultSpeed` |
| Parameters, locals | camelCase, no prefix | `targetPosition` |
| Booleans | verb prefix | `isDead`, `hasKey` |
| Methods | verb phrase; bool = question | `GetDirection`, `IsGameOver` |
| Enums | PascalCase singular; PascalCase values | `GameState.Playing` |
| Events | verb phrase; invoker `On…` | `DoorOpened`, `OnDoorOpened()` |
| Namespaces | PascalCase; mirror folders | `GridDungeon.Combat` |

## Fields and variables

- **Nouns** for fields, properties, and locals (they represent state or data). **Bools** are the exception: prefix with a verb that reads as a question — `isDead`, `isWalking`, `hasDamageMultiplier`, `canJump`.
- **Prefer properties** with a public getter over public fields when exposing state; use `[SerializeField]` on private fields for the Inspector instead of `public` fields.
- **Member prefixes**: `m_` private instance, `s_` static, `k_` constants; camelCase after the prefix (`m_movementSpeed`, not `m_MovementSpeed`). Locals and parameters stay unprefixed camelCase.
- Use **`this.`** when a field and local share a naming need; prefixes are preferred for members at a glance.
- **Default initialization**: numeric fields → `0`, references → `null`, bools → `false`. Omit redundant `= 0`, `= null`, `= false` unless required.
- **`protected`** when a subclass must access the field; do not widen to `public` later as a shortcut.
- **Access modifiers**: pick one team style — explicit `private` everywhere, or omit the default — and stay consistent.
- **No abbreviations** except single-letter loop/math names (`i`, `j`, `x`, `y`). Clarity beats brevity.
- **`var`**: only for locals when the type is obvious; avoid when it hides the type or for primitives outside tight loops.
- **One declaration per line.** Trim redundant type prefixes inside the owning type (`Player.m_score` → `m_score`).

## Enums

Enums are value types made of named constants. By default those constants are `int`, assigned from `0` upward.

- **PascalCase** for the enum type and every value.
- **Singular noun** for the type name — it holds one value from a set (`GameState`, not `GameStates`). No prefix or suffix on the type or values.
- **Global enums**: `public` enums may live outside a class when shared across assemblies.
- **`[Flags]` exception**: bitwise flag enums represent combined values — use a **plural** type name (`WeaponTypes`, `InputActions`) and document flag combinations.

```csharp
public enum GameState { Menu, Playing, Paused }

[Flags]
public enum InputActions { None = 0, Jump = 1, Attack = 2 }
```

## Classes and interfaces

- **Classes**: PascalCase **nouns** or noun phrases (`PlayerController`, `InventorySlot`) — types describe things; methods describe actions.
- **Role suffixes** (`*View`, `*Presenter`, `*Calculator`, …): see [unity-csharp-class-suffix-patterns](unity-csharp-class-suffix-patterns.mdc) and [class naming patterns](https://github.com/miramocha/griddungeon-design-docs/blob/main/docs/04-dev/class-naming-patterns.md).
- **Role suffixes** (`*View`, `*Presenter`, `*Calculator`, …): see [unity-csharp-class-suffix-patterns](unity-csharp-class-suffix-patterns.mdc) and [class naming patterns](https://github.com/miramocha/griddungeon-design-docs/blob/main/docs/04-dev/class-naming-patterns.md).
- **Interfaces**: `I` + PascalCase **adjective** describing capability (`IDamageable`, `IInteractable`).
- **MonoBehaviour files**: one `MonoBehaviour` per `.cs` file; the **filename must match** the behaviour class. Other helper types in the same file are fine. **Member section order**: `unity-csharp-formatting` (Fields → Properties → Events → lifecycle → public → private).

## Methods

In C#, callable code lives on **methods** (not standalone functions).

- The name should **reflect what it does** — readers should not need to open the body to guess behaviour.
- Start with a **verb** or verb phrase; add context when the short name is ambiguous — `GetDirection`, `FindTarget`, `ApplyDamage`.
- **Extra specificity** when the project has multiple conventions: `GetDistanceToTargetInMeters` beats `GetDistanceToTarget` if distances can be in cells, world units, or meters.
- **Parameters**: camelCase, same as locals (no `m_` prefix).
- **`bool` returns**: phrase as a yes/no question — `IsGameOver`, `HasStartedTurn`, `CanAfford`.

```csharp
float GetDistanceToTarget(Transform target) { … }
float GetDistanceToTargetInMeters(Transform target) { … } // when units vary in the project

public bool IsGameOver() => m_isDead;
void ApplyDamage(int amount, GameObject source) { … }
```

## Extension methods

Use extensions to add behaviour to types you **do not own** (often sealed Unity types) without subclassing. Keep them focused — a thin layer over `UnityEngine` APIs, not a home for unrelated helpers.

**Definition**

- `public static` method in a `public static class`
- First parameter: `this {Type} name` — the type being extended
- Container class name: **`{Type}Extensions`** (e.g. `TransformExtensions`), usually in its own file

**Naming**

- Same verb rules as ordinary methods — `ResetTransformation`, not `Reset` alone if unclear
- Method name describes the operation on the extended instance

```csharp
public static class TransformExtensions
{
    public static void ResetTransformation(this Transform transform)
    {
        transform.localPosition = Vector3.zero;
        transform.localRotation = Quaternion.identity;
        transform.localScale = Vector3.one;
    }
}

// usage
target.ResetTransformation();
```

- Prefer **instance methods on your own types** when you control the class; extensions are for third-party or sealed types.
- Null-check the `this` target when the API can receive null references.

## Events and event handlers

Events use the **observer pattern**: a subject (publisher) notifies subscribers without tight coupling. Pick one delegate style as a team and stay consistent.

### Event names

- **Verb phrase** in PascalCase that states the state change.
- **Present participle** ≈ about to happen (`OpeningDoor`); **past participle** ≈ already happened (`DoorOpened`).

### Delegates

- Prefer **`Action` / `Action<T>`** for most gameplay callbacks (void return, 0–16 parameters).
- `EventHandler` / `EventHandler<TEventArgs>` are fine when the team standardizes on them — do not mix styles ad hoc.

### Who names what

| Role | Naming | Example |
|------|--------|---------|
| Event field | verb phrase | `DoorOpened` |
| Publisher invoke method | `On` + event name | `OnDoorOpened()` |
| Subscriber handler (optional) | `{Subject}_` + event name | `GameEvents_DoorOpened` |

- **Custom `EventArgs`**: only when you need structured payload beyond `Action<T>` parameters; inherit `EventArgs` or use a dedicated struct.

```csharp
public event Action DoorOpened;

protected virtual void OnDoorOpened() => DoorOpened?.Invoke();

// subscriber
void GameEvents_DoorOpened() { … }
```

## Namespaces

Namespaces avoid clashes with the global namespace, other assemblies, and Asset Store packages.

- **PascalCase** segments separated by `.` — no underscores or special symbols (`GridDungeon.Combat`, not `grid_dungeon`).
- **Mirror folder structure** so layout matches code (`Assets/GridDungeon/Combat/` → `GridDungeon.Combat`).
- Use **sub-namespaces** for logical areas — `GridDungeon.GameFlow`, `GridDungeon.AI`, `GridDungeon.UI`.
- Put **`using`** directives at the top of the file to avoid repeating the full prefix.
- When two namespaces define the same short type name, **fully qualify** one side (`Player.Controller` vs `Enemy.Controller`) or alias with `using` — do not rely on ambiguous bare names.

```csharp
namespace GridDungeon.Combat
{
    public class DamageResolver { … }
}

// other file
using GridDungeon.Combat;
```

## Serialization (naming)

Inspector exposure and `[Serializable]` layout: see **`unity-csharp-formatting`**. Here: use `[SerializeField]` on private `m_` fields, not `public` serialized fields.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD
public float speed;

// ✅ GOOD
[SerializeField] float m_speed;
public float Speed => m_speed;
```

Match existing file style unless you are refactoring that area.
